## 4. How to run

### Option A — run everything via the orchestrator (recommended first pass)

```powershell
cd <folder containing all the scripts>
.\Run-DORAEvidenceCollection.ps1
```

This runs all nine scripts in sequence and writes output to
`.\DORA-Evidence-Output\`, including a merged `DORA-Evidence-MASTER.csv`.

Useful variations:

```powershell
# Target a specific subscription
.\Run-DORAEvidenceCollection.ps1 -SubscriptionId "11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555"

# Skip specific checks
.\Run-DORAEvidenceCollection.ps1 -Exclude PatchManagement,SecureScore

# Put output somewhere else
.\Run-DORAEvidenceCollection.ps1 -OutputFolder "C:\DORA-Evidence\2026-Q1"
```

You'll be prompted to sign in more than once — Microsoft Graph and Azure
Resource Manager are separate authentication contexts. In practice this
usually means one Graph sign-in prompt (reused across the Graph-based
scripts) and one Azure sign-in prompt (reused across the rest), not nine
separate prompts, but don't be alarmed if it asks twice.

### Option B — run an individual script

Every script is fully self-contained and independently documented (run
`Get-Help .\DORA-Evidence-BackupRecovery.ps1 -Full` for any of them to see
its own synopsis, parameters, and notes). For example:

```powershell
.\DORA-Evidence-BackupRecovery.ps1 -SubscriptionId "11111111-2222-3333-4444-555555555555"
```

Do this if you only care about one control area, or if you're troubleshooting
why a specific check in the orchestrator run looked wrong.

### Multiple subscriptions

None of these scripts iterate across subscriptions automatically (deliberately
— running broad, unscoped queries across an entire tenant by default is
usually the wrong default for a read-only evidence tool). For a
multi-subscription environment, loop the orchestrator yourself:

```powershell
$subs = Get-AzSubscription
foreach ($sub in $subs) {
    .\Run-DORAEvidenceCollection.ps1 -SubscriptionId $sub.Id -OutputFolder ".\DORA-Evidence-Output\$($sub.Name)"
}
```

(Conditional Access and Privileged Access are tenant/Graph-scoped, not
subscription-scoped, so they'll produce the same result each time through
this loop — that's expected, not a bug.)

