## 7. Troubleshooting

**"Could not retrieve" / empty results across a whole script** — almost
always a permissions gap. Re-check the Graph scopes or Azure RBAC role
listed in Section 3 above.

**PIM-related queries return nothing even though PIM is configured** —
check your Entra ID license tier (P2/Governance required); a P1 or Free
tenant will show this as "no PIM in use" even if PIM is set up.

**Defender/Secure Score checks show limited data** — check the Defender for
Cloud plan tier (Free vs. paid) on the resource types in question; Free tier
genuinely doesn't generate the same data these checks look for.

**Prompted to sign in more than twice** — this can happen if your PowerShell
session doesn't persist cached tokens between script invocations (varies by
OS/PowerShell version/module version). It's not harmful, just an extra
click.

**A specific role-management-policy finding in the PIM script looks wrong**
— this is the single most schema-fragile check across the whole set (see
that script's own header notes). Spot-check one role directly in the Entra
admin center (Privileged Identity Management > Azure AD roles > Settings)
before trusting the automated finding at scale.

---

## 8. Safety notes

- Every script authenticates interactively (a browser sign-in prompt) by
  default. No credentials are hardcoded or stored by any script.
- No script writes, creates, modifies, or deletes anything in your tenant.
  Every Graph/Azure cmdlet used is a `Get-*` (read) cmdlet.
- Run these on a machine with legitimate access to the tenant/subscription
  you're assessing, using an account with the minimum read-only permissions
  listed above — there's no reason to run this with anything more privileged
  than Reader/Security Reader/the specific Graph read scopes.
